This page is the human-readable face of a citable record: the archived dataset carries the driver,
the geometry, the cluster scripts, every raw result and the analysis that turns them into these
tables. It states what **peclet.flow {{flow_version}}** does on the hardware it was measured on. It is
not a comparison against other codes, and it makes no claim about any other version.

::: {.callout-note title="Amended 2026-09-15"}
The measurements are unchanged. Three readings of them were corrected after a companion campaign
(`benchmarks/momentum-solver`, archived alongside this record) re-ran the CPU ladder with the
momentum solver pinned: the {{switch_ranks}}-core gate difference is a **capped** momentum solve
against a converged one; the {{anom_n}}-GPU anomaly's decomposition-shape hypothesis is
**withdrawn**, contradicted by this record's own rungs; and the weak ladder's projection growth is
**not** the global reduction, which the timers put at {{w_top_ar_pct}} % of the phase. Each
correction is marked in place below. Nothing here revises a timing, an efficiency or the
permeability.
:::

## What is measured

**Creeping flow through a periodic random sphere packing, with the cut-cell immersed boundary
method**, driven by a body force in a triply periodic box. One MPI rank per GPU or per core.

The physical unit of the study is a **unit cell**: 1043 spheres of radius $R = 1$ at solid fraction
$\phi = {{phi}}$ in a cubic box of $21.333\,R$, resolved at $384^3$ cells — so $R = {{r_cells}}$ cells,
comfortably inside the resolution at which this discretization's permeability has converged. That
cell is {{cells_per_gpu_m}} M cells, and it is what one H100 holds.

Four ladders, one case:

| Ladder | Machine | What changes | Rungs |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Weak** | Snellius `gpu_h100` | one unit cell **per GPU**, the domain grows with the GPU count | 1 → {{w_top_n}} GPUs, {{cells_per_gpu_m}} M → {{w_top_gcells}} Gcells |
| **Strong (CPU)** | Snellius `genoa` | one fixed unit cell, more cores | {{s_base_n}} → {{s_top_n}} cores |
| **Strong (GPU)** | Snellius `gpu_h100` | one fixed unit cell, more GPUs | {{t_base_n}} → {{t_top_n}} GPUs |
| **Control** | both | Taylor–Green vortices, no geometry, same grids | subset |

The weak ladder **tiles one unit cell**. Rung $N$ is an exact periodic replication of the same
problem, $N$ times over — the porous-media analogue of tiling vortices. That choice is what makes
the physics observable exact rather than statistical: the tiled problem *is* the same problem, so
its answer must be the same number, and any disagreement is a defect rather than bed-to-bed
scatter.

## Correctness first

Every run starts from rest and takes the same {{gate_runs_steps}} steps, so the mean velocity
$\langle u \rangle$ at the end is one number that every rung must return — at any rank count, on
either machine. It costs one global reduction, and it is checked for every run in the record.

::: {.callout-important title="One state, {{gate_runs}} runs, two machines"}
$\langle u \rangle = {{gate_value}}$

across **{{gate_runs}} runs** spanning 1 to {{gate_max_ranks}} ranks, both the CUDA and the OpenMP
backend, single-rank and distributed, and domains from {{cells_per_gpu_m}} M to
{{gate_max_cells}} billion cells. Worst relative deviation **{{gate_worst}}**; peak divergence
{{gate_max_div}}. The geometry-free control is tighter still: **{{tgv_gate_worst}}** — machine
epsilon — over {{tgv_gate_runs}} runs to {{tgv_gate_max_ranks}} ranks.
:::

That is the claim the timings rest on. A fast rung that has quietly stopped solving the same
problem is worth nothing, so the equivalence is established first and the performance is read only
from runs that pass it.

**Where the record does *not* return one number, and why.** At {{switch_ranks}} cores this problem
is down to {{switch_cells_per_rank_k}} k cells per rank, and peclet 1.0.0 switches its momentum
solver by itself below ~65 k — so those rungs run the velocity multigrid where the rest of the
ladder does not. A different solver is a different iterate, and the two land
**{{switch_delta}}** apart in $\langle u \rangle$. That is a configuration difference rather than a
parallel-consistency one, and the evidence is that it is perfectly repeatable: {{switch_runs}}
separate {{switch_ranks}}-core allocations return values identical to the last digit
(spread {{switch_spread}}). The gate table groups by solver configuration for this reason.

::: {.callout-important title="Amended 2026-09-15: the two values are a capped solve and a converged one"}
A companion campaign (`benchmarks/momentum-solver`) re-ran this CPU ladder with the momentum
solver **pinned** at every rung instead of left to the automatic rule, and recorded the sweep count
the solver already reports and this record did not keep. On **every** rung of the CPU ladder the
red-black momentum solve runs **{{mom_cap}} sweeps per component — its iteration cap
(`velIters_`)** — while the velocity multigrid converges in {{mom_vcycles}}. So the
{{switch_delta}} above is not two converged solutions of the same equation reached by different
routes: it is a **capped** solve against a **converged** one, and the multigrid value is the
converged one.

Nothing in the timings below changes — they remain what peclet 1.0.0 does with its shipped
defaults, which is the question this record set out to answer. What changes is the reading: the
cross-rung equivalence gate demonstrates **reproducibility**, which it genuinely has to
{{gate_worst}}, and not convergence of the momentum solve. Whether the GPU ladders are also at
the cap was not measured here.
:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="All correctness gates, in full"}
{{< include gates.md >}}
:::

## Weak scaling — the distributed solve

![Per-GPU throughput and pressure iterations, 1 → {{w_top_n}} H100 GPUs at $384^3$ cells each.](weak_scaling.png)

Per-GPU throughput goes from **{{w_base_per}}** to **{{w_top_per}} Mcell/s** between 1 and
{{w_top_n}} GPUs: **{{w_weak_eff}} % weak efficiency** at {{w_top_gcells}} billion cells, an
aggregate {{w_top_throughput}} Mcell/s. The pressure solve takes **{{w_base_iters}} iterations per
step at 1 GPU and {{w_top_iters}} at {{w_top_n}}** — flat, which is the property that matters: a
weak ladder whose iteration count climbs is not weak-scaling, however level its throughput curve
looks. The multigrid hierarchy reaches {{levels}} levels and the coarse levels telescope onto
fewer ranks as they shrink, so the coarsest grid stays solvable however many ranks the fine grid
is spread over.

## Strong scaling — one fixed problem

![The same {{cells_per_gpu_m}} M-cell problem, on more cores and on more GPUs.](strong_scaling.png)

On CPU, {{s_base_n}} → {{s_top_n}} cores takes the step from {{s_base_s}} s to **{{s_top_s}} s**,
a **{{s_speedup}}×** speedup — nominally {{s_strong_eff}} % of ideal, which is above 100 % and
should not be read as the solver beating linear scaling. Two things inflate it, and both are worth
naming rather than banking. The {{s_base_n}}-core baseline has the whole node's memory bandwidth to
itself, so it is a flattered reference; and at {{switch_ranks}} cores the automatic rule switches
the momentum solver (above), so the top rung runs a *different and better* algorithm — 768 →
{{s_top_n}} alone returns 3.3× for a doubling. The honest reading is that this problem strong-scales
well across the whole CPU ladder, not that it exceeds ideal.

::: {.callout-important title="Amended 2026-09-15: how much of it was the solver switch"}
The companion pinned-solver ladder answers this quantitatively. Run with one algorithm from
{{mom_lo_n}} to {{mom_hi_n}} cores, the ladder reads **{{mom_sp_off}}×
({{mom_eff_off}} % of ideal) for red-black Gauss-Seidel** and **{{mom_sp_on}}×
({{mom_eff_on}} %) for the velocity multigrid** — each the honest scaling of a single algorithm,
and neither above 100 %. The {{s_strong_eff}} % figure above is what you get by measuring a capped
solver at the bottom of a ladder and a converged one at the top.

The same ladder says the multigrid is the better momentum solver **at every rank count**, not only
below the threshold where 1.0.0 switches to it: it wins by {{mom_solid_lo_ratio}}× to
{{mom_solid_hi_ratio}}× across the {{mom_solid_rungs}} rungs with repeat allocations, and its
margin is *largest* at the biggest blocks ({{mom_best_ratio}}× at {{mom_best_n}} cores,
{{mom_best_cells_k}} k cells per rank) — the opposite of the threshold's premise, and still
{{mom_best_ratio}}× ahead at {{mom_lo_cells_m}} M cells per rank. That is a defect in a shipped
default rather than a property of this benchmark, and it is recorded here because a reader sizing a
job from these numbers should know that `set_velocity_multigrid(True)` is worth roughly a factor of
two on CPU across this whole range.
:::

On GPU the same problem goes from {{t_base_s}} s on one H100 to **{{t_top_s}} s on {{t_top_n}}**
({{t_speedup}}×, {{t_strong_eff}} % of ideal). The efficiency falls off where it should: at
{{t_top_n}} GPUs each device holds only {{t_top_per_gpu_m}} M cells, far below what saturates an
H100, and the step becomes a latency problem rather than a throughput one. **That is the honest
limit of strong scaling on this hardware, and it is why the weak ladder is the headline** — a GPU
earns its keep on a large subdomain, and the fixed problem here fits on one of them to begin with.

One rung does not behave, and it is reproducible. At **{{anom_n}} GPUs the step is slower than at
{{anom_prev_n}}** ({{anom_ms}} ms against {{anom_prev_ms}} ms) — {{anom_repeats}} separate
allocations agree to {{anom_spread}}×, so this is not node placement. The phase timers put all of it
in the **projection**: {{anom_prev_proj}} → {{anom_proj}} ms while the momentum solve keeps scaling
({{anom_prev_mom}} → {{anom_mom}} ms), with an identical multigrid hierarchy at both rungs. The
decomposition shape is the obvious suspect — {{anom_prev_n}} GPUs give every rank a cubic block
({{block_8}}), {{anom_n}} give it a 1:2:2 one ({{block_16}}).

**That suspicion does not survive this record's own data, and is withdrawn (2026-09-15).** The
{{anom_next_n}}-GPU rung has a *more* elongated block ({{block_32}}) and a *worse* surface-to-volume
ratio ({{sv_32}} against {{sv_16}} at {{anom_n}} and {{sv_8}} at {{anom_prev_n}} —
`hierarchy_predict.txt`), and it is the **fastest** rung on the ladder at {{anom_next_ms}} ms. A
monotone quantity cannot produce a non-monotone dip. What the measurements do establish is a list
of what the anomaly is *not*: not node placement ({{anom_repeats}} allocations, different node
sets, agreeing to {{anom_spread}}×); not the momentum phase, which is on trend
({{anom_prev_mom}} → {{anom_mom}} → {{anom_next_mom}} ms); not the iteration count, flat at
{{t_base_iters}}; and not the global reduction, which the timers put at {{anom_ar_ms}} ms,
{{anom_ar_pct}} % of the projection. Nor is it the multigrid hierarchy: all three rungs build
{{levels}} levels, and the slow rung at {{anom_n}} GPUs and the fastest one at {{anom_next_n}}
have *identical* structure down to the level at which the coarse grids telescope onto fewer ranks
(L5 for both; {{anom_prev_n}} GPUs, the other fast rung, telescopes one level later — see
`hierarchy_predict.txt`). The two rungs that bracket the anomaly therefore differ from it in
opposite directions on every structural quantity available. The
anomaly is real, reproducible, confined to the projection, and undiagnosed.

Taken together the two machines price the same work: on this case one H100 is worth roughly
**{{cores_per_gpu}} Genoa cores**. That is the conservative reading — the CPU is credited with the
best per-core throughput anywhere on its ladder, which is its {{cores_per_gpu_rung}}-core rung, the
one that also benefits from the momentum-solver switch. Against its weakest rung the ratio is
several times larger.

## Where the step goes

![Per-phase median times across the weak ladder.](phase_breakdown.png)

The step is two solves and a negligible explicit stage (the predictor is {{ph_predictor_base}} ms,
about a tenth of a percent). Across the weak ladder the **momentum solve grows
{{ph_momentum_growth}} %** ({{ph_momentum_base}} → {{ph_momentum_top}} ms from 1 to {{w_top_n}}
GPUs) while the **pressure projection grows {{ph_projection_growth}} %**
({{ph_projection_base}} → {{ph_projection_top}} ms). The iteration count is flat, so the gap is
not a convergence effect.

**Amended 2026-09-15.** This record previously read that gap as "where the global coupling lives".
Its own timers do not support that: the pressure all-reduce is {{w_top_ar_ms}} ms at the top rung
against a {{ph_projection_top}} ms projection — **{{w_top_ar_pct}} %** — and its growth from
{{w_base_ar_ms}} ms accounts for a comparably small share of the increase. The global reduction is
not where the weak ladder loses. Two things that are: the V-cycle's coarse levels are
latency-bound, each costing a halo exchange on a grid too small to amortise it; and the hierarchy
**deepens along the ladder**, from {{w_base_levels}} levels at 1 GPU to {{w_top_levels}} at
{{w_top_n}}, because weak scaling grows the global grid — so part of that growth is extra levels
per V-cycle and is not a parallel inefficiency at all.

A second correction of emphasis. The projection carries the weak ladder's *loss*, but the momentum
solve carries the *time*: it is {{ph_momentum_share_base}} % of the step at 1 GPU and
{{ph_momentum_share_top}} % at {{w_top_n}}. Removing the projection's entire weak-scaling excess
would save less than halving the momentum solve would — and on CPU, pinning the momentum solver
does roughly that (above). Both statements are true and this record originally made only the
first.

## The geometry-free control

The Taylor–Green control runs the identical grids and the identical decomposition with no geometry
at all. It is **not** a measurement of what the immersed boundary costs, and should not be read as
one: the control solves an advecting flow while the bed case is creeping (advection off), so the
two differ in the physics as well as in the geometry.

What it does isolate is the solver floor and the shape of the scaling. Two things follow. The
cut-cell pressure operator needs **{{ibm_iters_ratio}}×** the iterations of the all-fluid one
({{w_base_iters}} against {{wt_base_iters}}), which is what a matrix whose coefficients span the
aperture contrast of a dense bed costs. And the whole step nevertheless lands within
**{{ibm_cost}}×** of the control's, because the control spends on advection roughly what the bed
spends on its stiffer pressure solve. The control also weak-scales slightly better
({{wt_weak_eff}} % against {{w_weak_eff}} % at {{w_top_n}} GPUs), which puts a number on how much
of the loss at the top of the ladder is the geometry's irregular work rather than the
communication both cases share.

## Permeability

Marched to steady state, the unit cell has $k/R^2 = {{k_over_R2}}$ ({{k_march_steps}} steps to a
relative tolerance of $10^{-5}$). Measured at {{k_runs}} points of the ladder, the estimates agree
to {{k_spread}} — the same physical answer from the single-GPU cell and from the tiled
{{w_top_gcells}}-billion-cell domain.

